<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  Pipeline-in-action chapter for the PROTEUS automation tool.
  Drop-in PreTeXt source. Cross-referenced via xml:id="ch-pipeline-demo".
  Uses five real responses from ula_shortanswers_MERGED.csv to illustrate
  the database state evolving through each pipeline stage.

  All codebook codes shown are synthetic placeholders (CODE_A, CODE_B,
  CODE_C), deliberately distinct from the canonical CS1-CS10 IDs; the
  demo is fixture data, not the codebook.
-->

<chapter xml:id="ch-pipeline-demo">
  <title>The Pipeline in Action</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The previous chapter described the pipeline at the level of design
      and intent. This chapter shows it running. We pick five real student
      responses from the pivot question on sheet <c>1.4.8</c> and walk
      each one through every stage, showing exactly what data the pipeline
      writes to the database after each step. By the end of the walkthrough
      the database contains rows in fourteen tables, the five responses
      have arrived at five different dispositions, and a reviewer can see
      the state of every response at every stage in a single query.
    </p>

    <p>
      The five responses were picked deliberately, one per disposition, so
      that the demo exercises every branch of the pipeline:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-cast">
      <title>The five demo responses and the disposition each is built to
      illustrate.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Tag</cell>
          <cell>Student</cell>
          <cell>What it illustrates</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>R1</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell>
          <cell>Clean single-criterion justification: <c>auto_accept</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>R2</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212l-003-11</c></cell>
          <cell>Blank response: <c>EXCLUDE</c> at Stage 2, then <c>escalate</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>R3</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell>Multi-criterion justification: <c>standard_review</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>R4</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell>
          <cell>Out-of-vocabulary phrasing: <c>terminology_flag</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>R5</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell>
          <cell>Chain extracted but nothing in the codebook fits: <c>no_match</c></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      The full text of each response appears at the relevant stage below.
      Codebook codes shown throughout this chapter are synthetic
      placeholders (<c>CODE_A</c>, <c>CODE_B</c>, <c>CODE_C</c>),
      deliberately distinct from the canonical codebook IDs
      <c>CS1</c>&#8211;<c>CS10</c>: the demo is fixture data illustrating
      the pipeline mechanics, not the codebook, and those mechanics do
      not depend on which IDs are in play.
    </p>

    <remark>
      <title>About the data shown</title>
      <p>
        The response texts in this chapter are verbatim from
        <c>ula_shortanswers_MERGED.csv</c>. The screening labels, chain
        steps, and pattern matches are illustrative: this chapter shows
        what the pipeline <em>would</em> write to the database, not the
        output of an actual prompt against a live model. The schema and
        the data shapes, however, are exactly what a working
        implementation produces.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Bootstrap                                           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-bootstrap">
    <title>Before the Pipeline Runs: Bootstrap Tables</title>

    <p>
      A few tables are populated before any response data is ingested.
      They establish the codebook version, the controlled vocabulary, and
      the registered reviewers. These tables exist mainly so that every
      downstream row knows which version of the codebook and vocabulary
      it was produced against.
    </p>

    <p>
      The codebook table (placeholder content):
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-codebook">
      <title>Initial state of the <c>codebook</c> table.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>code_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>version_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>name</c></cell>
          <cell><c>description</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>CODE_A</c></cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>Free variables (placeholder)</cell>
          <cell>Uses presence of free variables to classify the solution set.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>CODE_B</c></cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>Pivot structures (placeholder)</cell>
          <cell>Uses count or existence of pivots to classify the solution set.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>CODE_C</c></cell>
          <cell>1</cell>
          <cell>Relating two countables (placeholder)</cell>
          <cell>Compares two countable features (e.g. <m>\#\text{vars}</m> vs. <m>\#\text{pivots}</m>).</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      And the controlled vocabulary the chain extractor will produce its
      output in:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-vocab">
      <title>Initial state of the <c>vocabulary</c> table.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>term_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>canonical_form</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>1</cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell><c>more_vars_than_pivots</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>3</cell><cell><c>no_unique_solution</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>4</cell><cell><c>system_consistent</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell>5</cell><cell><c>infinite_solutions</c></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Two reviewers are registered (<c>geoff</c> and <c>ayla</c>). A new
      pipeline run is then started, which writes a single row to the
      <c>pipeline_runs</c> table tagged with the codebook version, the
      vocabulary version, the prompt version, and the model identifier.
      Every row the pipeline writes from this point forward references
      this run's ID, so the entire output of one run is recoverable from
      a single key.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Stage 1                                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-stage-1">
    <title>Stage 1: Ingest</title>

    <p>
      The DataShop <c>.tab</c> file is read, short-answer events for the
      target question are extracted, and one row per response is written
      to the <c>responses</c> table. The full raw file is recorded in
      <c>raw_imports</c>, with its SHA-256 hash for de-duplication.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each <c>response_id</c> is a deterministic hash of
      <m>(\textit{student\_id}, \textit{question\_id}, \textit{timestamp})</m>,
      truncated to sixteen characters. The hash means re-importing the
      same file produces the same row IDs, so re-imports are idempotent
      rather than destructive.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-stage-1">
      <title>State of the <c>responses</c> table after Stage 1.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>response_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>student_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>response_text</c> (truncated)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>a1f3...c2e9</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell>
          <cell>There is free variable so it is not possible to have unique solution.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>b88e...4071</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212l-003-11</c></cell>
          <cell><em>(blank)</em></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>3d22...90a4</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell>One and only one solution is not possible because there will be a free variable since there are more variables than rows. We can only have 4 pivot positions at max.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>7f01...88bc</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell>
          <cell>It depends what is in the augmented matrix</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>5cd9...e417</c></cell>
          <cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell>
          <cell>Its the same because as we've discussed in class only 4 pivot points would be available.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Stage 2                                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-stage-2">
    <title>Stage 2: Eligibility Screening</title>

    <p>
      Each response is sent to the screener, which returns a label, a
      confidence, and a one-sentence rationale. The result is written to
      the <c>screenings</c> table. The original responses are not modified.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-stage-2">
      <title>State of the <c>screenings</c> table after Stage 2.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>student_id</c></cell>
          <cell><c>label</c></cell>
          <cell><c>confidence</c></cell>
          <cell><c>rationale</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell>0.96</cell>
          <cell>Connector ("so") + named criterion ("free variable").</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212l-003-11</c></cell>
          <cell><c>EXCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell>0.99</cell>
          <cell>Empty response.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell>0.95</cell>
          <cell>Multiple connectors and multiple named criteria.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell>0.71</cell>
          <cell>Hedging response with conditional reasoning; criterion is meta-level.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell>0.83</cell>
          <cell>Connector ("because") + numerical reference ("4 pivot points").</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      <c>R2</c> exits the pipeline here. Its row in <c>responses</c> is
      preserved; no further processing is attempted on it. Stage 5 will
      eventually write a <c>disposition</c> row of <c>escalate</c> for it,
      so that it appears in the human review queue with a clear note that
      the screener excluded it. The other four responses move on to
      Stage 3.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Stage 3                                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-stage-3">
    <title>Stage 3: Reasoning Chain Extraction</title>

    <p>
      For each <c>INCLUDE</c>'d response, the chain extractor produces a
      numbered list of inferential steps in the controlled vocabulary.
      Each step has the form <em>premise &#x2192; conclusion</em>; the
      premise is null for steps that introduce an observation rather than
      derive one. Steps that contain a term not present in the controlled
      vocabulary are flagged with <c>oov_flag = 1</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each chain produces one row in <c>chains</c> and several rows in
      <c>chain_steps</c>. After Stage 3, the chain-related tables look
      like the following.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-stage-3">
      <title>State of the <c>chain_steps</c> table after Stage 3, joined
      with <c>chains</c> and <c>responses</c> for readability.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>student</c></cell>
          <cell><c>step</c></cell>
          <cell><c>premise</c></cell>
          <cell><c>conclusion</c></cell>
          <cell><c>oov</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>&#x2014;</cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell><cell>2</cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell><cell><c>no_unique_solution</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>&#x2014;</cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>&#x2014;</cell><cell><c>more_vars_than_pivots</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell><cell>3</cell><cell><c>more_vars_than_pivots</c></cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell><cell>4</cell><cell><c>free_var_exists</c></cell><cell><c>no_unique_solution</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>&#x2014;</cell><cell><c>solution_depends_on_b</c></cell><cell>1</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>&#x2014;</cell><cell><c>only_4_pivots_available</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell><cell>2</cell><cell><c>only_4_pivots_available</c></cell><cell><c>same_solution_set</c></cell><cell>0</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Two things are worth flagging in this output.
    </p>

    <p>
      <c>R3</c>'s chain has four steps because the student's response
      contains two parallel arguments: a count-comparison argument
      (<q>more variables than rows</q>) and a free-variable argument
      (<q>there will be a free variable</q>). The chain extractor
      represents these as a sequence rather than as parallel branches; the
      matching stage will pick up patterns at any step.
    </p>

    <p>
      <c>R4</c> produces a single OOV-flagged step. The student's response
      (<q>It depends what is in the augmented matrix</q>) is not making a
      claim about pivots, free variables, or solution set cardinality &#x2014;
      it is hedging the entire question on the contents of <m>b</m>. The
      controlled vocabulary has no proposition for <q>solution depends on
      <m>b</m></q>, so the extractor invents one and flags it as out of
      vocabulary. This is the signal that Stage 5 will later use to route
      the response for terminology review.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Stage 4                                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-stage-4">
    <title>Stage 4: Pattern Matching</title>

    <p>
      For each chain step, the matcher iterates over every codebook entry
      and reports each pattern that fires. There is no preference rule and
      no winner-take-all logic: every match is recorded. Matches are
      written to the <c>matches</c> table, keyed by step.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-stage-4">
      <title>State of the <c>matches</c> table after Stage 4.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>student</c></cell>
          <cell><c>step</c></cell>
          <cell><c>code</c></cell>
          <cell><c>rationale</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell><c>CODE_A</c></cell>
          <cell>Free variable used to constrain solution set.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell><c>CODE_C</c></cell>
          <cell>Compares <m>\#\text{vars}</m> and <m>\#\text{rows}</m>.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell><c>CODE_A</c></cell>
          <cell>Free variable rules out unique.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell>4</cell>
          <cell><c>CODE_B</c></cell>
          <cell>Pivot count classifies solution set.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      <c>R4</c> and <c>R5</c> have no rows in the <c>matches</c> table.
      For <c>R4</c>, the chain's only step is OOV, and OOV terms cannot
      match codebook patterns by construction. For <c>R5</c>, the steps
      are in vocabulary but no codebook pattern fires: the student's
      reasoning relies on a partial pivot-count observation that does not
      reach a control-structure-grade conclusion. Both situations are
      handled at Stage 5.
    </p>

    <p>
      <c>R3</c> produces three matches across two steps. Step 4 in
      particular fires two different codes (<c>CODE_A</c> and <c>CODE_B</c>)
      because the same proposition can be read through two lenses: as a
      free-variable argument or as a pivot-count argument. The matcher
      reports both and lets Stage 5 raise the situation for human review
      rather than guessing which the student meant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: Stage 5                                             -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-stage-5">
    <title>Stage 5: Disposition</title>

    <p>
      For each response, Stage 5 looks at what the previous stages produced
      and decides where the response should go: directly accepted, sent
      for ordinary review, flagged for a specific reason, or escalated to
      full manual coding. The decision is written to the <c>dispositions</c>
      table. Stage 5 introduces no new analysis; it is a small set of
      rules over the structured outputs of Stages 2&#x2013;4.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-stage-5">
      <title>State of the <c>dispositions</c> table after Stage 5.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>student</c></cell>
          <cell><c>screening</c></cell>
          <cell><c>disposition</c></cell>
          <cell><c>trigger_note</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell><c>auto_accept</c></cell>
          <cell>Single clean match.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212l-003-11</c></cell>
          <cell><c>EXCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell><c>escalate</c></cell>
          <cell>Excluded at Stage 2.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell><c>standard_review</c></cell>
          <cell>3 distinct codes matched across 4 chain steps.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell><c>terminology_flag</c></cell>
          <cell>OOV term, no matches.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell>
          <cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell>
          <cell><c>no_match</c></cell>
          <cell>Chain extracted but no codes matched.</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- SECTION: End-to-end summary                                  -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <section xml:id="sec-demo-summary">
    <title>End-to-end Trace</title>

    <p>
      The five responses now occupy rows in seven different tables (more if
      you count the bootstrap and provenance tables): <c>responses</c>,
      <c>screenings</c>, <c>chains</c>, <c>chain_steps</c>, <c>matches</c>,
      <c>dispositions</c>, and the join to <c>pipeline_runs</c>.
      A reviewer who wants the state of every response at every stage in
      one place can write a single SQL query that joins these tables.
      <xref ref="tab-demo-end-to-end"/> shows what such a query returns.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-demo-end-to-end">
      <title>End-to-end trace, one row per response.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell><c>student</c></cell>
          <cell><c>screening</c></cell>
          <cell><c>steps</c></cell>
          <cell><c>matches</c></cell>
          <cell><c>disposition</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-09</c></cell><cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell><c>auto_accept</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212l-003-11</c></cell><cell><c>EXCLUDE</c></cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><c>escalate</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-003-22</c></cell><cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell><cell>4</cell><cell>3</cell><cell><c>standard_review</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-002-10</c></cell><cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><c>terminology_flag</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>212I-001-07</c></cell><cell><c>INCLUDE</c></cell><cell>2</cell><cell>0</cell><cell><c>no_match</c></cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      This is the row a reviewer's dashboard would render. Clicking any
      row would expand it to show the full chain, the full set of matches,
      and the rationale at each stage. Every cell in this table is
      derivable from one query against the schema; nothing is computed at
      display time.
    </p>

    <aside xml:id="aside-demo-q">
      <title>What this exercise revealed</title>
      <p>
        <em>The schema needs composite foreign keys for any reference to
        the codebook.</em> Because <c>codebook</c> uses a composite primary
        key <m>(\textit{code\_id}, \textit{version\_id})</m>, any table
        that references it (<c>matches</c>, <c>review_codes</c>) must
        carry both columns. This was caught the first time the demo
        attempted to insert a <c>matches</c> row.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>The pipeline writes more rows than one might expect.</em> Five
        responses produced one row in <c>raw_imports</c>, one in
        <c>pipeline_runs</c>, five in <c>responses</c>, five in
        <c>screenings</c>, four in <c>chains</c>, nine in <c>chain_steps</c>,
        four in <c>matches</c>, and five in <c>dispositions</c> &#x2014;
        thirty-four rows total, in addition to the twelve bootstrap rows.
        For a real run of two hundred responses this scales to roughly
        fifteen hundred rows, which is comfortably within SQLite's
        capabilities.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>The dispositions cleanly separate.</em> Five responses, five
        different terminal dispositions. The pipeline does not collapse
        cases that need different reviewer attention into a single bucket.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Re-running the pipeline does not destroy old runs.</em> Every
        row produced by the pipeline references a <c>run_id</c>. A second
        run on the same responses would produce a parallel set of rows in
        <c>screenings</c>, <c>chains</c>, <c>chain_steps</c>,
        <c>matches</c>, and <c>dispositions</c>, leaving the first run's
        output intact. This is what allows prompt-version A and prompt-
        version B to be compared on the same corpus.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</chapter>
